ompliance, and operational requirements. Workspace admins can manage who can access which meetings, restrict access by team or role, and configure sharing permissions at both workspace and meeting level. This helps companies keep sensitive conversations visible only to the people who need them. MeetGeek encrypts recordings and transcripts at rest and in transit using enterprise-grade security standards. This protects meeting data while it is stored, processed, and transmitted across the platform. You can delete individual meetings or your account from your settings. Once deleted, the data is permanently removed according to MeetGeek’s data deletion policy and applicable retention requirements. MeetGeek does not sell meeting data to advertisers or external organizations. Data is shared with third-party integrations only when authorized by the user or workspace admin, and only for the purpose of enabling the connected workflow. Enterprise teams can configure retention rules, archive policies, deletion timelines, and access expiration settings. This gives larger organizations more control over how long meeting data is stored and how it is governed across departments. MeetGeek is designed to scale with your workspace without forcing every user into the same paid plan. You can grow your team, invite unlimited free users to collaborate in the workspace, and control which plan is assigned to each user. This gives admins more flexibility over cost, access, and scalability as the company expands. The free plan mainly limits the number of transcription minutes available each month. It is still useful for individuals and teams because free users can participate in a workspace, access shared meeting knowledge, and experience key MeetGeek functionality without a credit card, commitment, or upfront payment. You can start for free, test the product in real workflows, and decide whether a paid plan is needed based on actual usage. Paid plans unlock higher transcription limits, more advanced AI capabilities, custom templates, integrations, collaboration features, automation options, longer retention, and support levels depending on the plan. The main difference is not only more usage, but more control over how meeting intelligence is structured, shared, automated, and connected to the rest of your workflow. MeetGeek offers a 14-day trial for paid features. This gives you two weeks to test the full product experience before committing to a subscription. Annual billing can save around 40% compared to monthly billing. For teams that expect to use MeetGeek continuously, annual billing is usually the more cost-efficient option. Eligible startups, registered nonprofits, and accredited educational institutions may qualify for special pricing. Teams can apply through MeetGeek’s discount program or contact the sales team to confirm eligibility. When you downgrade, your past meetings and data become subject to the retention policy and limits of the new plan: 1 month (Basic), 6 months (Pro), or 1 year (Business). Contact our support team to confirm the exact retention period for your plan. Enterprise plans include advanced admin controls, custom data retention, SSO, dedicated account management, SLA options, assisted onboarding, stronger governance features, and support for larger organizational rollouts. Enterprise is designed for companies that need more control, compliance, scalability, and implementation support. MeetGeek can connect with CRMs such as HubSpot and Salesforce to push meeting summaries, action items, notes, and relevant call insights into the correct deal, company, or contact record. This helps sales teams keep CRM data updated without manually rewriting what happened in every call. MeetGeek can sync action items into tools such as Notion, ClickUp, Asana, and other connected systems. When action items are detected, they can be pushed into the appropriate workspace, project, database, or task list, with relevant context from the meeting and attribution when available. You can configure sharing rules that route specific meeting outputs to the right Slack channels. For example, sales call summaries can go to a sales channel, customer feedback can go to a product channel, and team updates can go to an internal operations channel. MeetGeek connects to your Google Calendar or Outlook calendar to detect upcoming meetings and apply your recording rules. Depending on your setup, MeetGeek can dispatch the bot, prepare the recording flow, or help automate your meeting capture process without requiring manual setup each time. Native integrations are purpose-built connections with specific tools, offering a deeper and more direct experience. Zapier connections let you connect MeetGeek with 10,000+ apps through no-code trigger-action workflows. They are useful when you want to build workflows beyond MeetGeek’s native integration library. You can use Make, Zapier, or n8n to connect MeetGeek with the rest of your stack and build multi-step automations around meeting data. For example, you can extract names, emails, decisions, objections, customer requests, internal blockers, or follow-up ideas from a meeting and route them into different systems. This can support workflows across CRM, support, project management, documentation, reporting, customer success, recruiting, operations, and internal knowledge management. MeetGeek can save recordings, transcripts, and summaries to a designated Google Drive folder. Depending on your configuration, files can be organized by date, meeting type, workspace, or other rules that help your team keep meeting records accessible outside MeetGeek. You can configure what gets pushed into connected tools. Depending on the integration, this can include summaries, transcripts, action items, highlights, meeting notes, decisions, analytics, or selected structured fields. The goal is to let each team decide what information should move from the meeting into the tools where work actually happens. Developers can use the MeetGeek API to pull transcripts, summaries, action items, highlights, analytics, and other meeting outputs into custom dashboards, internal tools, proprietary workflows, or company-specific systems. This allows MeetGeek to become part of a broader meeting intelligence layer inside the company’s infrastructure. Yes. MeetGeek offers a dedicated MCP server that allows MCP-compatible tools to access MeetGeek workspace data. Teams can use the hosted option or self-host the MCP server, depending on their technical and security preferences. With MCP, users can connect MeetGeek to tools such as Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor, VS Code agents, and other compatible clients, making it possible to ask questions about meeting data or build workflows on top of MeetGeek from third-party AI tools. You sign up, connect your Google or Outlook calendar, choose your recording preferences, and configure your workspace based on your role and needs. From that point, MeetGeek is ready to support your next meetings and starts adapting to how you work: what meetings you attend, what outputs matter, what tools you connect, and how you want meeting intelligence to flow through your workspace. The web app is the main hub for reviewing meetings, managing settings, searching your meeting history, configuring templates, and connecting integrations. The desktop app enables bot-free recording directly from your computer and can support online, in-person, and hybrid conversations. The browser extension supports browser-based recording and can be useful when you want a lightweight way to capture meetings without relying on a bot. The mobile app supports in-person recording, field conversations, and on-the-go review. Some capabilities are cross-app, so different parts of the workflow can be controlled from different MeetGeek applications. The bot appears as a named participant in your meeting, typically as “MeetGeek Notetaker.” On paid plans, you can customize the bot’s display name so it better matches your company, team, or meeting context. For individual use, IT approval is usually not required. For company-wide deployment, IT admins may need to approve calendar access, OAuth permissions, domain-level settings, security requirements, integrations, or data governance rules depending on your organization’s policies. Workspace admins can manage users, roles, recording permissions, sharing rules, integrations, billing, retention policies, and access controls from a central workspace dashboard. This helps larger teams roll out MeetGeek consistently while maintaining control over privacy, compliance, and internal workflows. MeetGeek requests access to calendar events so it can detect upcoming meetings, apply recording rules, and support automatic capture. Calendar access helps MeetGeek understand when and where meetings happen, but it does not modify, delete, or create calendar events unless explicitly configured to do so through supported workflows. You can set inclusion and exclusion rules based on meeting title, participants, calendar, platform, or other conditions.This allows MeetGeek to record the meetings that matter while staying out of private, sensitive, or irrelevant conversations. Yes. If the meeting was recorded with another tool or by another participant, you can upload the audio or video file to MeetGeek. MeetGeek will process the file and generate the transcript, summary, highlights, and action items as it would for a live recording. During or after onboarding, you can configure summary templates, sharing rules, integrations, notification preferences, recording rules, and automation triggers from your workspace settings. This means MeetGeek can be ready to produce structured outputs and trigger workflows from your first recorded meeting. Enterprise teams can access dedicated onboarding support, structured rollout planning, live training, priority support, documentation, and implementation guidance. This helps larger organizations deploy MeetGeek across teams while aligning security, adoption, integrations, and workflow automation from the beginning.",
