    "content": "Turn ordinary footage or text prompts into editable 3D animation data—without suits, markers, or a multi-camera studio. Start Free Markerless AI Motion Capture — Convert Video or Text into Editable 3D Motion QuickMagic estimates full-body, hand, and facial movement from a single video — or generates motion from a text prompt — and converts it into editable 3D animation data for your character workflows. No suits, reflective markers, sensors, or multi-camera studio required. Export to FBX, BVH, BIP, C4D, VMD, Mixamo, UE4, UE5.5, UE5.6, Character Creator & iClone, Roblox, OnlyFace, and UniRobot for Blender, Unreal Engine, Unity, Maya, 3ds Max, MotionBuilder, MikuMikuDance, and more. Export availability varies by plan and workflow. Turn Video into Editable 3D Motion Upload footage recorded with a phone, webcam, or camera. QuickMagic estimates body, hand, and facial movement and converts it into editable animation data for your character workflows. Results depend on subject visibility, motion blur, occlusion, camera movement, and the type of action being captured. Supports: Full-body and upper-body capture Hand and facial motion options Single- and multi-subject workflows Static or moving-camera footage Pose, frame-rate, and motion-cleanup controls Try Video Motion Capture Create Human Motion References for Humanoid Robots Generate human-motion references from video or text for simulation, imitation-learning experiments, behavior prototyping, and humanoid-robot workflows. Export options currently include presets for supported Unitree robot models. Supports: Human-motion references for simulation Imitation-learning and behavior prototyping Video- or text-based motion input Unitree G1, H1, and H1_2 motion presets Human motion data for research and testing workflows Explore Robot Motion Data Generate 3D Motion from a Text Prompt Describe an action, pose, or character performance in plain language. QuickMagic creates an editable motion draft that you can preview, export, and refine in your animation pipeline. Supports: Storyboards and previs Game and animation prototypes Character performance ideation MMD, VTuber, and digital-human content Editable motion drafts for refinement Create Motion from Text Export Motion to Your 3D Pipeline Every Major 3D Tool Use QuickMagic motion data in Blender, Unreal Engine, Unity, Maya, 3ds Max, MotionBuilder, Cascadeur, iClone, MikuMikuDance, Roblox, and other animation workflows. Export to FBX, BVH, BIP, C4D, VMD, Mixamo, UE MetaHuman, Character Creator & iClone, Roblox, OnlyFace, and UniRobot. Export availability varies by plan and workflow. Also supported: Cascadeur, UEFN (Fortnite), Cartoon Animator, and other 2D/3D pipelines. See How Creators Use QuickMagic Explore real workflows from artists and developers who use QuickMagic for Unreal Engine characters, Cascadeur cleanup, MMD videos, iClone animation, and UEFN projects. Frequently Asked Questions About AI Motion Capture What is AI motion capture? AI motion capture uses computer vision and machine-learning models to estimate human movement from video and convert it into 3D animation data. Unlike traditional optical capture, it does not require reflective markers or a dedicated multi-camera studio. How does QuickMagic turn a video into 3D motion? Upload a supported video, select the subject and capture options, and let QuickMagic estimate movement frame by frame. You can then preview the result and export an available animation format for further editing in your 3D software. What type of video gives the best results? Use clear footage with the subject visible, adequate lighting, limited motion blur, and minimal long-term occlusion. Full-body framing is recommended for full-body capture. Complex floor contact, fast spins, or close interaction between people may require additional cleanup. Does QuickMagic support hand and facial motion? QuickMagic provides body, hand, and facial-motion options for supported workflows. The amount of usable detail depends on image quality, hand and face visibility, motion blur, and the selected output workflow. Can QuickMagic capture more than one person? QuickMagic supports multi-subject workflows for suitable footage. For better results, keep each person visible and avoid prolonged overlap or leaving the frame. Confirm the current subject limit in the product interface. Can I use footage from a moving camera? Moving-camera footage is supported for selected workflows, but camera shake, rapid viewpoint changes, and occlusion can reduce stability. Use the appropriate tracking mode and review global movement before export. Which export formats and 3D tools are supported? QuickMagic supports FBX and a range of workflow-specific export presets for popular 3D tools. Available formats vary by plan and product version. See the Export Formats page for the current compatibility table. Is QuickMagic free? QuickMagic offers a free plan for trying AI motion capture. Current limits, export formats, queue priority, video length, storage, and paid-plan features are listed on the Pricing page. How long does processing take? Processing time varies with video length, resolution, selected features, queue load, and plan priority. The product interface shows the current job status. Can I use QuickMagic motion commercially? Commercial-use rights depend on the plan and on the rights you hold to the uploaded footage, characters, audio, and other source materials. Review the current User Agreement before using generated assets in commercial work. How long are uploaded files and generated assets stored? The current Pricing page lists 15-day asset storage for the Free plan and ongoing account storage for paid plans. Storage and data-retention rules are also described in the Privacy Policy. Can QuickMagic generate motion for humanoid robots? QuickMagic provides motion-reference workflows and presets for selected humanoid robot models. Check the Robot Motion Data page for supported robots, joint definitions, coordinate systems, and current limitations.",
