 tools, approvals, publishing, and reporting in one workflow, so campaigns move faster without extra headcount. Colors, fonts, voice, products, customers, and approved examples stay available every time Riley plans or creates. Designs, video, copy, ads, and captions come back as campaign assets, not loose drafts you have to rebuild. Owners, review status, and approvals stay visible before anything moves to a channel. Approved posts move to the right destination with the schedule, format, and campaign context attached. Results feed the next plan, so last week's campaign becomes next week's brief. Run marketing yourself, manage client work, or let your own agents call the execution layer through MCP and APIs. Give each client its own brand, channels, calendar, and approvals. Switch workspaces without rebuilding the process. Let your agent use Simplified to create, approve, publish, and report without leaving your workflow. All in one place Simplified keeps design, content, and social scheduling under one roof, so daily marketing takes fewer handoffs. Less tool switching The value is not only creating content faster. It is spending less time moving assets, captions, approvals, and publishing details between tools. Built for repeat work Recurring social content and campaign tasks feel more manageable when last week's work can become next week's plan. Easy to keep moving It helps us move from idea to approval without chasing files, owners, or status across different tools. Faster campaign work Planning, creative, captions, and scheduling are easier to manage when one team can own the whole campaign run. Cleaner client reviews Keeping drafts, approvals, and publishing status in one place makes client review smoother and easier to track. Keep Claude, Codex, Cursor, or your own agent. Give it access to brand context, creative tools, approvals, publishing, and reports. MCP is the open standard AI agents use to connect to outside tools. Connect Claude, Codex, Cursor, or any MCP-compatible client to the marketing layer Riley uses. Let your agent create media, schedule posts, and pull analytics from commands it can run. REST, OAuth2, and webhooks for custom workflows that still respect brand rules and approval gates. We'd ask them too. Not unless you tell it to. Review is the default: work queues as drafts and you approve or reject it. Auto-publish is opt-in, and you can pause everything instantly from the app, Slack, or the CLI. It reads your Brand Kit and your approved past posts, then adjusts every time you approve or reject a draft. The longer you use it, the closer it gets. You do. Copy, designs, video, captions, and campaign assets belong to your account, and you keep them even if you cancel. No. You can start with Riley in plain English. MCP, CLI, and API access are optional for teams that want to connect their own agents. It cannot publish it unless you approved it or turned on auto-publish for that workflow. One tap rejects a bad draft, and the team learns from the rejection. Simplified is free to start with no credit card required. Credit amounts and usage vary by plan, and the product shows the credit cost before generation so you know what each action uses. Start with Riley, or give Claude, Codex, Cursor, and your own agents a real execution layer.",
